252,50002e5f-6de4-46ec-9da3-e5810fd0fede,268,en,events|node_type|76710fc4-3157-498e-ba85-0501ed7ed1ac,"2026-02-04T21:01:31+00:00|Y-m-d\TH:i:sP",1|user|85298f8d-c10c-44ac-af32-f23600324c58|/user/1,,1,1|user|85298f8d-c10c-44ac-af32-f23600324c58|/user/1,"Sam's Jams @ Bobby's Idle Hour Tavern","2026-02-04T20:56:43+00:00|Y-m-d\TH:i:sP","2026-02-04T21:46:59+00:00|Y-m-d\TH:i:sP",,,1,1,"meta|title|Sam's Jams @ Bobby's Idle Hour Tavern | Open Jam Events|meta|robots|index, follow|link|canonical|https://openjamevents.com/sams-jams-bobbys-idle-hour",/sams-jams-bobbys-idle-hour|137|en,,,Wednesdays,"An open forum for aspiring songwriters",,"9 Music Square S, Nashville, TN 37203","12 noon - 8 PM","Sam's Jams at Bobby's Idle HourEvery Wednesday from noon until 8 PM, Bobby's Idle Hour on Music Row hosts Sam's Jams—one of Nashville's most popular daytime songwriter showcases. Led by host Sam Cooper, this afternoon writers session attracts everyone from emerging songwriters testing new material to hit writers stopping by between studio sessions.The Vibe: Bobby's Idle Hour occupies a tiny, historic space on Music Row that feels transported from 1950s Nashville. There are maybe four card-table-sized tables down the middle, a bar, and chairs along walls completely covered in photos of famous musicians who've played here over the decades. The menu is famously simple—no kitchen, just a microwave, offering pizza, pot pies, and Vienna sausages (listed at ""market price"" and served with crackers and mustard).The vibe is ""old school Nashville"" in the best way possible. This isn't polished or corporate—it's a genuine hangout where songwriters gather to share songs, swap stories, and be part of Music Row's creative community. On Wednesdays, that energy intensifies as Sam Cooper curates an all-day showcase that attracts both industry professionals and music lovers seeking authentic songwriter rounds.The space is so small and intimate that there's no separation between performers and audience. Everyone sits close, conversations are whispered during songs, and the focus stays squarely on the music. Tour buses occasionally stop outside, but regulars and industry folks keep the atmosphere grounded and real.Performances: Sam's Jams runs from noon to 8 PM—an unusually long window that provides flexibility for writers with different schedules. The format includes both advance-booked performers and occasional walk-in slots if the schedule has gaps.Booking is preferred rather than first-come, first-served. Contact Sam Cooper (256-335-3790) the Sunday before you want to play to secure a guaranteed time block, typically 3-4 songs. Day-of walk-ins should call, text, or arrive as early as possible to see if Sam can fit them in, though slots aren't guaranteed.This advance-booking system maintains quality and flow while still allowing spontaneity. The result is a high-demand afternoon where serious songwriters perform for knowledgeable audiences including publishers, fellow writers, and dedicated music fans.The Wednesday afternoon slot attracts different demographics than evening showcases. Industry professionals can stop by during work hours, retirees and music lovers have daytime flexibility, and the all-day format means you can catch a set during lunch or spend the entire afternoon immersed in Nashville's songwriting community.Bobby's Idle Hour also hosts Thursday Writers Night (7 PM, hosted by Janet and Charles Cox—one of Nashville's longest-running writers nights anywhere) and Monday Comedy Night (sign up at door, 7-9 PM). This programming makes the venue a Music Row institution despite its humble appearance.For songwriters, playing Sam's Jams means performing in front of people who understand the craft. This isn't tourists looking for something to do—it's the Nashville songwriting community supporting each other, learning from each other, and occasionally doing business together.The semi-cheap beer, intimate setting, and genuine commitment to songwriting make Bobby's Idle Hour feel like a creative sanctuary in an increasingly commercialized music industry. Sam Cooper's curation ensures quality without elitism, creating an afternoon where great songs matter more than who wrote them or whether they've charted.|basic_html|Sam's Jams at Bobby's Idle HourEvery Wednesday from noon until 8 PM, Bobby's Idle Hour on Music Row hosts Sam's Jams—one of Nashville's most popular daytime songwriter showcases. Led by host Sam Cooper, this afternoon writers session attracts everyone from emerging songwriters testing new material to hit writers stopping by between studio sessions.The Vibe: Bobby's Idle Hour occupies a tiny, historic space on Music Row that feels transported from 1950s Nashville. There are maybe four card-table-sized tables down the middle, a bar, and chairs along walls completely covered in photos of famous musicians who've played here over the decades. The menu is famously simple—no kitchen, just a microwave, offering pizza, pot pies, and Vienna sausages (listed at ""market price"" and served with crackers and mustard).The vibe is ""old school Nashville"" in the best way possible. This isn't polished or corporate—it's a genuine hangout where songwriters gather to share songs, swap stories, and be part of Music Row's creative community. On Wednesdays, that energy intensifies as Sam Cooper curates an all-day showcase that attracts both industry professionals and music lovers seeking authentic songwriter rounds.The space is so small and intimate that there's no separation between performers and audience. Everyone sits close, conversations are whispered during songs, and the focus stays squarely on the music. Tour buses occasionally stop outside, but regulars and industry folks keep the atmosphere grounded and real.Performances: Sam's Jams runs from noon to 8 PM—an unusually long window that provides flexibility for writers with different schedules. The format includes both advance-booked performers and occasional walk-in slots