e from tourists experiencing bluegrass for the first time to legends like Ricky Skaggs and Alison Krauss who've sat in when passing through town.For musicians, playing the Sunday jam at Station Inn represents connection to bluegrass history. This is where the tradition lives—not in a museum or on recordings, but in living, breathing musical exchange between generations. Beginners learn by following along, experienced players share knowledge freely, and everyone benefits from the collective energy of acoustic instruments swelling together in a small room.The Station Inn has been called the ""bluegrass version of Preservation Hall in New Orleans""—a comparison that captures both the venue's historical importance and its continuing vitality. On Sunday nights, this legendary room fulfills its mission perfectly: preserving and celebrating bluegrass music in its most authentic, communal form.Come early, bring your instrument (if you play), order some pizza and beer, and experience American roots music the way it was meant to be shared—acoustic, democratic, joyful, and alive with the sound of voices and strings raised together in celebration of a tradition that refuses to die.","36.152869855936|-86.783417201014||36.152869855936, -86.783417201014",,,,,,"Station Inn",
250,d1129916-364d-4a09-b18b-39d247e28e24,266,en,events|node_type|76710fc4-3157-498e-ba85-0501ed7ed1ac,"2026-02-04T20:47:19+00:00|Y-m-d\TH:i:sP",1|user|85298f8d-c10c-44ac-af32-f23600324c58|/user/1,,1,1|user|85298f8d-c10c-44ac-af32-f23600324c58|/user/1,"Open Mic @ Tennessee Brew Works","2026-02-04T20:44:10+00:00|Y-m-d\TH:i:sP","2026-02-04T21:35:38+00:00|Y-m-d\TH:i:sP",,,1,1,"meta|title|Open Mic @ Tennessee Brew Works | Open Jam Events|meta|robots|index, follow|link|canonical|https://openjamevents.com/open-mic-tennessee-brew-works",/open-mic-tennessee-brew-works|136|en,,,Mondays,"Relaxed low key open mic for beginners and professionals alike",,"809 Ewing Ave, Nashville, TN 37203","5:30 PM - 8:00 PM","Open Mic Mondays at Tennessee Brew WorksEvery Monday evening, Tennessee Brew Works transforms from an award-winning craft brewery into one of Nashville's most accessible and welcoming open mic venues. Unlike the competitive intensity of some Music City songwriter showcases, this open mic celebrates the joy of performance in a relaxed, brewery atmosphere where craft beer and original music flow equally.The Vibe: Tennessee Brew Works opened in 2013 as Nashville's first production brewery, located in the SoBro (South of Broadway) neighborhood. The venue occupies a renovated warehouse with exposed brick, industrial design, and an open brewery floor where massive fermentation tanks provide the backdrop for performances.The downstairs performance area creates an intimate but unpretentious atmosphere. There's no velvet rope, no audition process, no lottery system—just first-come, first-served signups starting at 5:30 PM. The crowd includes brewery regulars enjoying Monday night pitcher specials ($5 off craft beer pitchers during open mic hours), tourists exploring Nashville beyond Broadway, and local music lovers supporting the songwriting community.This is explicitly a singer-songwriter open mic, focused on original material. The venue proudly bills itself as an ""Official Nashville Live Music Venue"" with live music at least six days a week, making Monday nights part of a broader commitment to supporting local musicians.Performances: Signups begin at 5:30 PM with performances running from 6:00-8:00 PM. The format is straightforward—each performer gets a slot to showcase their original songs. The venue provides PA system and microphones, but musicians bring their own instruments.The relaxed two-hour window keeps energy high without the marathon sessions that plague some open mics. Performers and audience members alike appreciate the tight schedule—everyone gets stage time, and the event wraps at a reasonable hour.What distinguishes Tennessee Brew Works is the integration of food, drink, and music. The venue serves a beer-centric menu until late evening, with happy hour from 3-6 PM featuring $6.99-$8.99 bites. The brewery specializes in craft beers incorporating Tennessee ingredients, with styles ranging from classic lagers to experimental ales.All ages are welcome (though alcohol service follows legal requirements), making this a family-friendly option rare among Nashville music venues. Parents can bring kids to experience live music, beer enthusiasts can sample the latest experimental brews, and songwriters can perform for an audience that's genuinely engaged but not intimidatingly critical.There's no cover charge for performers or listeners, making this one of Nashville's most accessible weekly music events. The venue's location just south of downtown provides easy access from multiple neighborhoods while avoiding the tourist crush of Lower Broadway.For songwriters new to Nashville, Tennessee Brew Works offers an ideal introduction to the city's music scene—professional enough to take seriously, casual enough to not feel overwhelming, and supportive enough that even nervous first-timers feel welcomed. For established local songwriters, it provides a testing ground for new material and a Monday night hangout with fellow musicians over excellent beer.The combination of award-winning craft beer, quality sound system, welcoming atmosphere, and genuine appreciation for original songwriting makes Open Mic Mondays at Tennessee Brew Works a cornerstone of Nashville's grassroots music community.|basic_html|Open Mic Mondays at Tennessee Brew WorksEvery Monday evening, Tennessee Brew Works transforms from an award-winning craft brewery into one of Nashville's most accessible and welcoming open mic venues. Unlike the competitive intensity of some Music City songwriter showcases, this open mic celebrates the joy of performance in a relaxed, brewery atmosphere where craft beer and original music flow equally.The Vibe: Tennessee Brew Works opened in 2013 as Nashville's first production brewery, located in the SoBro (South of Broadway) neighborhood. The venue occupies a renovated warehouse with exposed brick, industrial design, and an open brewery floor where massive fermentation tanks provide the backdrop for performances.The downstairs performance area creates an intimate but unpretentious atmosphere. There's no velvet rope, no audition process, no lottery system—just first-come, first-served signups starting at 5:30 PM. The crowd includes brewery regulars enjoying Monday night pitcher specials ($5 off craft beer pitchers during open mic hours), tourists exploring Nashville beyond Broadway, and local music lovers supporting the songwriting community.This is explicitly a singer-songwriter open mic, focused on original material. The venue proudly bills itself as an ""Official Nashville Live Music Venue"" with live music at least six days a week, making Monday nights part of a broader commitment to supporting local musicians.Performances: Signups begin at 5:30 PM with performances running from 6:00-8:00 PM. The format is straightforward—each performer gets a slot to showcase their original songs. The venue provides PA system and microphones, but musicians bring their own instruments.The relaxed two-hour window keeps energy high without the marathon sessions that plague some open mics. Performers and audience members alike appreciate the tight schedule—everyone gets stage time, and the event wraps at a reasonable hour.What distinguishes Tennessee Brew Works is the integration of food, drink, and music. The venue serves a beer-centric menu until late evening, with happy hour from 3-6 PM featuring $6.99-$8.99 bites. The brewery specializes in craft beers incorporating Tennessee ingredients, with styles ranging from classic lagers to experimental ales.All ages are welcome (though alcohol service follows legal requirements), making this a family-friendly option rare among Nashville music venues. Parents can bring kids to experience live music, beer enthusiasts can sample the latest experimental brews, and songwriters can perform for an audience that's genuinely engaged but not intimidatingly critical.There's no cover charge for performers or listeners, making this one of Nashville's most accessible weekly music events. The venue's location just south of downtown provides easy access from multiple neighborhoods while avoiding the tourist crush of Lower Broadway.For songwriters new to Nashville, Tennessee Brew Works offers an ideal introduction to the city's music scene—professional enough to take seriously, casual enough to not feel overwhelming, and supportive enough that even nervous first-timers feel welcomed. For established local songwriters, it provides a testing ground for new material and a Monday night hangout with fellow musicians over excellent beer.The combination of award-winning craft beer, quality sound system, welcoming atmosphere, and genuine appreciation for original songwriting makes Open Mic Mondays at Tennessee Brew Works a cornerstone of Nashville's grassroots music community.","36.150498931106|-86.775533744745||36.150498931106, -86.775533744745",,,,,257|node|cf419b55-5275-4891-8fbe-a146ba1bf342|/tennessee-brew-works,"Tennessee Brew Works",
247,e217a9eb-8e90-4d2b-be04-356450977d1a,262,en,events|node_type|76710fc4-3157-498e-ba85-0501ed7ed1ac,"2026-02-04T20:36:25+00:00|Y-m-d\TH:i:sP",1|user|85298f8d-c10c-44ac-af32-f23600324c58|/user/1,,1,1|user|85298f8d-c10c-44ac-af32-f23600324c58|/user/1,"Open Mic @ The Bluebird Cafe","2026-02-04T20:28:23+00:00|Y-m-d\TH:i:sP","2026-02-04T21:31:39+00:00|Y-m-d\TH:i:sP",,,1,1,"meta|title|Open Mic @ The Bluebird Cafe | Open Jam Events|meta|robots|index, follow|link|canonical|https://openjamevents.com/open-mic-the-bluebird-cafe-nashville",/open-mic-the-bluebird-cafe-nashville|133|en,,,Monday,"Open Mic (Monday) - Writers Nights (Sunday) - Featured Songwriter Shows",,"4104 Hillsboro Pike, Nashville, TN 37215","6PM - 7:45PM","Monday Open Mic Night at The Bluebird CafeFor songwriters worldwide, Monday nights at the Bluebird Cafe represent something approaching the holy grail of open mic opportunities. This isn't just another bar hosting people with guitars—it's a legendary listening room where almost every country music star has performed, where recording industry professionals scout talent, and where the competitive Monday open mic serves as the gateway to the venue's more prestigious performance slots.The Vibe: The Bluebird opened in 1982 in a Green Hills strip mall, and its unassuming exterior belies its massive influence on Nashville's songwriting community. The venue pioneered the ""in-the-round"" format where songwriters sit facing each other in the center of the room, trading songs and stories. This intimate arrangement creates unique energy—audience members sit close enough to see every expression, hear every whispered aside, and feel the vulnerability that comes with sharing original material.Thanks to the ABC television show ""Nashville,"" which featured the Bluebird prominently, the venue has become even more popular with tourists and industry alike. On Monday nights, that energy intensifies. This is serious business—songwriters come to showcase their best work, hoping to catch the attention of publishers, producers, or simply earn the respect of their peers.The venue maintains a strict ""quiet policy"" during performances. This isn't background music—people come to listen, and conversations are not tolerated during songs. The respect for the craft creates an environment where every word matters.Performances: Sign-ups open at 11 AM Central on Monday mornings via the Bluebird's website. Competition is fierce—there are typically more people trying to sign up than available slots, so not everyone succeeds in securing a spot. This online lottery system replaced the old first-come, first-served format but hasn't diminished the competitive nature.Each participating songwriter gets to perform one original song, solo or accompanied by no more than two others. The emphasis is on originals only—covers are not permitted. Performance time runs from 6:00-7:45 PM, with performers cycling through alphabetically.What makes the Bluebird open mic special is its role as the first step in the venue's performer development pathway. Musicians who perform well at open mics and demonstrate commitment can audition for Sunday Writers Nights (held quarterly). Those who excel at Sunday nights may eventually be selected for Early Shows (Tuesday-Saturday at 6 PM), and the very best may graduate to the coveted Late Shows (Tuesday-Saturday at 9 PM) reserved for hit songwriters.There's no cover charge for the Monday open mic, making it accessible to any songwriter willing to brave the competitive signup process. The Bluebird operates on a two-item minimum for food and beverages, which is standard for the venue.For out-of-town songwriters making pilgrimages to Music City, Monday open mic represents a chance to play the same stage where Taylor Swift, Garth Brooks, and countless other legends got their start. For Nashville residents, it's a weekly opportunity to test new material in front of the most discerning audience in the music industry.The Monday open mic isn't for the faint of heart, but for serious songwriters committed to their craft, there's no more prestigious or potentially career-changing open mic in America.|basic_html|Monday Open Mic Night at The Bluebird CafeFor songwriters worldwide, Monday nights at the Bluebird Cafe represent something approaching the holy grail of open mic opportunities. This isn't just another bar hosting people with guitars—it's a legendary listening room where almost every country music star has performed, where recording industry professionals scout talent, and where the competitive Monday open mic serves as the gateway to the venue's more prestigious performance slots.The Vibe: The Bluebird opened in 1982 in a Green Hills strip mall, and its unassuming exterior belies its massive influence on Nashville's songwriting community. The venue pioneered the ""in-the-round"" format where songwriters sit facing each other in the center of the room, trading songs and stories. This intimate arrangement creates unique energy—audience members sit close enough to see every expression, hear every whispered aside, and feel the vulnerability that comes with sharing original material.Thanks to the ABC television show ""Nashville,"" which featured the Bluebird prominently, the venue has become even more popular with tourists and industry alike. On Monday nights, that energy intensifies. This is serious business—songwriters come to showcase their best work, hoping to catch the attention of publishers, producers, or simply earn the respect of their peers.The venue maintains a strict ""quiet policy"" during performances. This isn't background music—people come to listen, and conversations are not tolerated during songs. The respect for the craft creates an environment where every word matters.Performances: Sign-ups open at 11 AM Central on Monday mornings via the Bluebird's website. Competition is fierce—there are typically more people trying to sign up than available slots, so not everyone succeeds in securing a spot. This online lottery system replaced the old first-come, first-served format but hasn't diminished the competitive nature.Each participating songwriter gets to perform one original song, solo or accompanied by no more than two others. The emphasis is on originals only—covers are not permitted. Performance time runs from 6:00-7:45 PM, with performers cycling through alphabetically.What makes the Bluebird open mic special is its role as the first step in the venue's performer development pathway. Musicians who perform well at open mics and demonstrate commitment can audition for Sunday Writers Nights (held quarterly). Those who excel at Sunday nights may eventually be selected for Early Shows (Tuesday-Saturday at 6 PM), and the very best may graduate to the coveted Late Shows (Tuesday-Saturday at 9 PM) reserved for hit songwriters.There's no cover charge for the Monday open mic, making it accessible to any songwriter willing to brave the competitive signup process. The Bluebird operates on a two-item minimum for food and beverages, which is standard for the venue.For out-of-town songwriters making pilgrimages to Music City, Monday open mic represents a chance to play the same stage where Taylor Swift, Garth Brooks, and countless other legends got their start. For Nashville residents, it's a weekly opportunity to test new material in front of the most discerning audience in the music industry.The Monday open mic isn't for the faint of heart, but for serious songwriters committed to their craft, there's no more prestigious or potentially career-changing open mic in America.","36.102262473857|-86.816392431198||36.102262473857, -86.816392431198",,,,,249|node|bbefbf93-ded9-4d33-8eb6-07592940d1a1|/the-bluebird-cafe%20-%20nashville-tn,"The Bluebird Cafe",
